      `Maryam bint Imran (peace be upon her) holds the supreme station among all women in Quranic revelation. She is the only woman explicitly mentioned by name in the Holy Quran, referenced 34 times across multiple chapters—more frequently than in the New Testament itself. An entire majestic chapter (Surah Maryam, Surah 19) bears her blessed name. In Surah Ali 'Imran, the angels address her with the highest accolade bestowed upon female creation: "O Maryam, indeed Allah has chosen you and purified you and chosen you above the women of the worlds" (3:42).`,
      `Before her birth, her mother (the wife of Imran, traditionally Hannah) dedicated the child in her womb exclusively to the service of Allah\'s sanctuary: "My Lord, indeed I have dedicated to You what is in my womb, consecrated [for Your service], so accept this from me" (3:35). Expecting a male who could minister in the Temple, she gave birth to a female and named her Maryam, praying for divine protection for her and her descendants from Satan. Allah accepted Maryam with gracious acceptance, placing her under the guardianship of the prophet Zakariyya. Within her secluded prayer chamber (Mihrab), Maryam spent her days and nights in profound devotion, miraculously receiving out-of-season fresh fruits directly from Allah.`,
      `Her supreme trial and miracle occurred when the archangel Jibril appeared before her in the form of a handsome human being. Fearing for her chastity, Maryam immediately sought refuge in the Divine: "Indeed, I seek refuge in the Most Merciful from you, [so leave me] if you fear Allah" (19:18). Jibril announced that he was a messenger conveying the gift of a pure boy (Isa) through direct divine command, without human intervention. Conceived through the divine breath, Maryam experienced the heavy burden of social stigma and physical travail, withdrawing to a distant place under the shade of a date palm.`,
      `In the agony of labor, isolated and anticipating the slander of her community, she cried out in human vulnerability: "Oh, I wish I had died before this and was in oblivion, forgotten!" (19:23). Divine mercy enveloped her immediately. A voice called from beneath her, bidding her not to grieve: a fresh stream of clear water was caused to flow at her feet, and she was commanded to shake the trunk of the palm tree so that ripe, fresh dates would rain down upon her. She was instructed to observe a fast of silence (Sawm as-Samt), speaking to no human being.`,
      `Returning to her people carrying the newborn infant, she was met with vicious accusations: "O Maryam, you have certainly done a thing unprecedented! O sister of Harun, your father was not a man of evil, nor was your mother unchaste!" (19:27–28). Bound by her fast of silence, Maryam simply pointed to the infant in the cradle. Miraculously, the newborn Isa spoke, exonerating his mother's immaculate honor, proclaiming her purity to the assembly, and declaring his prophetic mission. Allah commemorated Maryam as Al-Siddiqah (The Truthful Saint) and the eternal archetype of absolute faith, chastity, and obedience to God.`
